# Code Quality

- Keep comments focused on why, not how, aim for self-explanatory code.
- When refactoring, remove old comments, never add comments about what changed.
- Never document old behavior or behavior changes in code.

# Error Handling

Follow Ousterhout's "A Philosophy of Software Design", minimize exceptions.

- Define errors out of existence where possible.
- Crash on genuinely unexpected errors.
- Don't catch-and-recover from internal errors, fix the bug.
- Validate at system boundaries only.
- try-finally for resource cleanup is fine.

# Debugging

- Find the root cause. Never fix symptoms or add workarounds.
- Read error messages carefully.
- Reproduce consistently before investigating.
- Form a single hypothesis, test minimally, verify before continuing.
- If a fix doesn't work, stop and re-analyze.

# Verifying a Change

- Vary one thing. A different machine/GPU shifts float output and reads as a regression, so re-run the OLD code on the SAME machine before believing the change caused a number to move.
- A comparator that cannot fail proves nothing. Perturb one field of one record by 1e-9 and confirm the comparator reports it, then trust "outputs are identical".
- Diffing JSON outputs: NaN != NaN, so a plain `x != y` walk flags every NaN column. Records can also carry different key sets, so walk `set(a) | set(b)` with `.get(k, sentinel)`.
- A threshold calibrated at one span is not span-free. Control: IQR over N randomly drawn samples stays flat as N grows, IQR over a contiguous window of the same N rises. If those two diverge the quantity is autocorrelated and the threshold owns a span.
